  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Monday...\n\nA weak shortwave in the flow will bring a low (<20%) chance for\nshowers and weak thunderstorms over the Beartooth/Absaroka\nmountains and foothills this afternoon and over the Bighorn\nmountains and foothills this evening, moving east into the plains\nalong the Montana/Wyoming border overnight.\n\nWednesday into Thursday, upper level ridging builds over the \nregion, keeping conditions dry and bringing a warming trend. Highs\nwill increase from 80s Wednesday to 90s Thursday. Sometime \nThursday into Friday, the ridge begins to break down and a mostly \ndry cold front moves through. Breezy winds of 20 to 30 mph will\naccompany the frontal passage, although there is uncertainty in if\nthe front moves through Thursday afternoon or Thursday night. In \neither case, this will limit highs on Friday to the 90s.\n\nSaturday into early next week, the ridge continues to flatten,\nwith highs decreasing from mid to upper 90s over the weekend to\nupper 80s to low 90s by Monday. As the ridge flattens, additional\nperiods of breezy conditions may occur through the weekend. By \nMonday, monsoonal moisture moves into the region. Along with weak \nshortwaves in the flow, this may lead to daily afternoon and \nevening showers and thunderstorms (20-50% chance) early next week.\n\nWith hot and dry conditions expected Thursday through the weekend,\nalong with the potential for breezy winds, fire weather concerns \nare elevated. Take care not to cause a spark!\n\nSmoke will continue to create hazy skies at times through the\nweek. As the ridge begins to break down and flatten, turning upper\nlevel flow out of the west, more smoke may move into the region\nand affect air quality and visibility. For the latest air quality,\nvisit airnow.gov. Archer",
